const CC_STUDIOS=[{"id":"cc-teach","title":"Build the teach-back loop","description":"Sequence a conversation from a manageable explanation to a repaired understanding gap.","mode":"sequence","evidence":"teachback","lesson":"teachback-skill","why":"A check becomes useful when an explanation gap leads to repair.","when":"Before a follow-up or information conversation.","how":"Select the five moves in order, check your sequence and read the feedback.","steps":["Explain one next step in plain language.","Invite the person to explain it in their own words.","Listen and identify the understanding gap.","Re-explain the unclear part in a different way.","Invite teach-back again to check the repair."],"intro":"The person describes a different next step from the one intended. Build the full loop, including the repair."},{"id":"cc-risk","title":"See the absolute difference","description":"Move two fictional risks and compare an absolute difference with a relative change.","mode":"risk","evidence":"engagement","lesson":"risk-evidence","why":"Relative percentages can sound large without revealing a baseline.","when":"When preparing to explain a study or compare two options.","how":"Adjust the fictional figures, calculate the change, and compare your answer with the visual model."},{"id":"cc-reconcile","title":"Spot the transfer discrepancy","description":"Compare two fictional lists, classify the differences and route the unresolved questions.","mode":"match","evidence":"medication","lesson":"med-transitions","why":"A list mismatch must be verified rather than turned into an assumed prescription.","when":"Before practising an approved reconciliation workflow.","how":"Compare the records and classify each row; inspect the debrief before saving.","rows":[["Medicine A","Listed as current","Not listed","Omission / status to verify"],["Medicine B","Listed once","Listed twice","Possible duplicate to verify"],["Medicine C","Listed as current","Listed as current","No visible discrepancy in these records"]],"options":["Omission / status to verify","Possible duplicate to verify","No visible discrepancy in these records"]},{"id":"cc-bias","title":"Catch the first-story trap","description":"Work through three reasoning moments and seek information that could challenge an assumption.","mode":"decisions","evidence":"harm","lesson":"humility","why":"A plausible first explanation can obscure a mismatch.","when":"During a fictional case review or peer discussion.","how":"Choose a response at each moment and compare the reasons for all options.","scenes":[["The early label","A fictional team has an early explanation. A new observation does not fit.","What should happen next?",["Name the mismatch and revisit alternatives with the responsible team.","Set the new information aside.","Ask only for evidence confirming the first explanation."],0,["The mismatch should inform reassessment and appropriate review.","This preserves the first explanation without examining new evidence.","Confirming evidence alone can reinforce an incomplete explanation."]],["The confident colleague","A senior colleague sounds certain, but the relevant source has not been checked.","What makes the discussion more useful?",["Accept certainty as proof.","Ask which observations support the view and what would change it.","Wait until no uncertainty remains before discussing it."],1,["Confidence alone does not establish a claim.","This invites the evidence and its limits into the conversation.","Uncertainty can be communicated and managed now."]],["The updated view","Review changes the fictional team’s working explanation.","What closes the loop?",["Keep the update among the people in the room.","Treat the earlier uncertainty as a personal failure.","Communicate the revised view and next action to the relevant people."],2,["Relevant others may still be working with the earlier plan.","Updating a view is part of careful reasoning.","An updated explanation must reach those who need to act."]]]},{"id":"cc-verify","title":"Trace the AI claim","description":"Inspect a fictional citation, its population and a proposed sharing workflow.","mode":"decisions","evidence":"digital","lesson":"data-stewardship","why":"A fluent claim can still be unsupported, irrelevant or unsafe to share.","when":"Before relying on digital material in an approved workflow.","how":"Choose what to check at each stage, then inspect your complete decision trail.","scenes":[["A polished reference","A fictional AI draft names an unfamiliar study but gives no retrievable source.","What is the useful next check?",["Locate the actual publication and inspect the claim independently.","Ask the same model to promise accuracy.","Treat the citation’s formatting as verification."],0,["Independent checking tests whether the source and support exist.","Reassurance from the same system is not independent evidence.","Presentation does not establish that a reference is real."]],["A different population","You locate a real paper. Its participants and outcome differ from the draft.","What should change?",["Use it because the journal is real.","Do not generalise the claim; examine relevance and seek appropriate review.","Remove the population details to simplify the claim."],1,["A real paper may not support this particular claim.","Relevance is necessary in addition to source existence.","Removing context makes the claim harder to assess."]],["An unsafe shortcut","A colleague suggests uploading a note without its name to an unapproved public tool.","What is appropriate?",["Upload it if the answer is useful.","Assume removing a name makes it anonymous.","Use the approved information-governance workflow; other details may identify someone."],2,["Usefulness does not authorise disclosure.","Other details and combinations can identify someone.","Purpose, authorisation and the full information content matter."]]]},{"id":"cc-access","title":"Make participation possible","description":"Spot an access barrier, ask the person and adapt the interaction.","mode":"decisions","evidence":"access","lesson":"dignity","why":"The usual format may create a barrier that is not immediately visible.","when":"When preparing a conversation, appointment or learning session.","how":"Explore three fictional moments, choose an adaptation and review the reasoning.","scenes":[["A quiet agreement","A fictional person nods during an unfamiliar-language explanation.","What is the useful next step?",["Ask about preferred language and arrange appropriate support.","Assume the nod confirms understanding.","Ask a nearby child to translate clinical details."],0,["A direct preference check and appropriate support improve access.","Agreement alone does not reveal understanding.","Use qualified interpretation through the service where needed."]],["An inaccessible format","The person says the standard written sheet is difficult to use.","How should you respond?",["Give a second copy of the same sheet.","Ask what format or support works for them and arrange it through the service.","Infer the person’s ability from appearance."],1,["Repeating the same format may repeat the barrier.","The person’s expressed needs should shape the adaptation.","Appearance cannot establish what support is useful."]],["A companion speaks","A companion answers everything while the person is present.","What supports participation?",["Address all questions only to the companion.","Assume the person does not want involvement.","Speak directly to the person and ask how they want the companion involved."],2,["This can leave the person’s own voice unheard.","Ask rather than assume their preference.","Direct address and a preference check preserve participation."]]]},{"id":"cc-interrupt","title":"Recover after an interruption","description":"Build a deliberate return to a fictional administrative task after competing demands.","mode":"sequence","evidence":"interruptions","lesson":"human-factors","why":"An interruption can leave task status and responsibility unclear.","when":"When reflecting on interruptions in non-clinical learning work.","how":"Sequence the four recovery moves, then consider which system support would help.","intro":"Fictional task: preparing a learning pack. An interruption leaves you unsure which checks were completed. This sequence is not a clinical procedure.","steps":["Pause and identify the task’s current status.","Verify completed and outstanding steps against the approved task record.","Clarify uncertainty or ownership before continuing.","Resume from the verified point and record the next action."]},{"id":"cc-compassion","title":"Support with warmth and limits","description":"Practise empathetic responses, honest boundaries and a practical support route.","mode":"decisions","evidence":"wellbeing","lesson":"compassion-boundaries","why":"Support is more dependable when it acknowledges the person and your role.","when":"Before a peer-support or workload conversation.","how":"Choose a response in three moments, then review the action and the boundary.","scenes":[["The first response","A fictional colleague says the workload feels unmanageable.","Which response opens a useful discussion?",["That sounds difficult. What is the most pressing concern right now?","Everyone is busy.","A one-minute reset will solve it."],0,["Acknowledgment and an open question invite the person’s actual concern.","Comparison can close the discussion.","A short reset does not resolve continuing workload conditions."]],["A realistic offer","You can listen now but cannot change the rota yourself.","What can you say?",["I guarantee your workload will change.","I can listen and help identify the appropriate person to discuss the workload.","I will take sole responsibility for fixing everything."],1,["Avoid promising an outcome outside your control.","This combines a useful action with an honest boundary.","An unlimited promise can create an unreliable support plan."]],["Beyond a short pause","The strain is persistent and affecting concentration.","What should the response include?",["Only another breathing exercise.","Keeping it private in every circumstance.","Appropriate support and a workload or safety discussion through the local route."],2,["Continuing strain may need more than an immediate pause.","Appropriate trusted support may be important.","A response should consider the person and working conditions."]]]}];
const CC_EVIDENCE={"harm":{"title":"Preventable harm across care settings","numbers":[["6%","pooled prevalence of preventable harm"],["70","studies; 337,025 patients"]],"kind":"bar","ratio":6,"source":"Panagioti et al. · BMJ · 2019","url":"https://www.bmj.com/content/366/bmj.l4185","type":"Systematic review","finding":"Preventable harm was reported across multiple care settings.","population":"70 observational studies; 337,025 patients across medical care settings.","context":"Pooled estimate 6% (95% CI 5–7%). Rates and definitions varied between studies. This is historical synthesis, not a local incident rate or Carecraft outcome."},"handoff":{"title":"A structured handoff programme","numbers":[["23%","relative reduction in medical errors"],["30%","relative reduction in preventable adverse events"]],"kind":"bar","ratio":23,"source":"Starmer et al. · NEJM · 2014","url":"https://www.nejm.org/doi/abs/10.1056/NEJMsa1405556","type":"Multicentre before-and-after study","finding":"Error rates fell after implementation of the I-PASS bundle.","population":"Nine paediatric residency programmes; 10,740 patient admissions.","context":"A multicomponent intervention, not an SBAR-only effect. Before-and-after associations do not isolate every cause or predict this app’s effect."},"medication":{"title":"Medication reconciliation at transitions","numbers":[["2.85","discrepancies per patient at the start"],["0.98","discrepancies per patient at the end"]],"kind":"compare","ratio":34.3859649122807,"source":"Schnipper et al. · BMJ Quality & Safety · 2022","url":"https://qualitysafety.bmj.com/content/31/4/278","type":"Multicentre quality-improvement study","finding":"Discrepancy rates fell during the MARQUIS2 implementation.","population":"18 North American hospitals or hospital systems, with data collection at 17; 2016–2018.","context":"Observed rates across an 18-month, mentored, multicomponent intervention. A discrepancy is not synonymous with injury. No individual tool effect or dose decision is inferred."},"ipc":{"title":"A bundle for catheter-related infection","numbers":[["Up to 66%","reduction in infection rates"],["103","ICUs reporting data"]],"kind":"bar","ratio":66,"source":"Pronovost et al. · NEJM · 2006","url":"https://www.nejm.org/doi/abs/10.1056/NEJMoa061115","type":"Collaborative cohort study","finding":"A coordinated infection-prevention programme was associated with sustained improvement.","population":"Michigan intensive-care units; catheter-related bloodstream infections; 18-month study.","context":"Historical, multicomponent ICU programme. This is not the effect of hand hygiene alone, a general infection rate, or proof that a Studio prevents infections."},"access":{"title":"Accessibility is a routine need","numbers":[["28.7%","of US adults reported functional disability"],["~70m","US adults, based on 2022 data"]],"kind":"bar","ratio":28.7,"source":"CDC · Disability and Health Data Now · 2025","url":"https://www.cdc.gov/disability-and-health/articles-documents/disability-and-health-data-now.html","type":"Population surveillance","finding":"Accessible services matter to a substantial part of the adult population.","population":"2022 US Behavioral Risk Factor Surveillance System, reported by CDC.","context":"US self-reported survey data, not global or Saudi prevalence. Definitions differ from other surveys. The figure does not reveal any individual’s needs: ask the person."},"wellbeing":{"title":"Burnout and reported safety incidents","numbers":[["2.04","odds ratio for patient-safety incidents"],["170","studies in the full review"]],"kind":"none","source":"Hodkinson et al. · BMJ · 2022","url":"https://www.bmj.com/content/378/bmj-2022-070442","type":"Observational meta-analysis","finding":"Physician burnout was associated with higher odds of safety incidents.","population":"239,246 physicians in the full review. The overall-burnout/safety analysis used 35 studies and 41,059 physicians.","context":"OR 2.04 (95% CI 1.69–2.45); substantial heterogeneity. Odds are not risk. Association does not establish causation or the effect of an individual wellbeing exercise."},"engagement":{"title":"Decision aids improve understanding","numbers":[["+11.9","points on a 100-point knowledge scale"],["209","studies in the complete review"]],"kind":"none","source":"Stacey et al. · Cochrane · 2024","url":"https://www.cochrane.org/evidence/CD001431_patient-decision-aids-help-people-who-are-facing-decisions-about-health-treatment-or-screening","type":"Review of randomised trials","finding":"Decision aids improved knowledge compared with usual care.","population":"107,698 adults in the full review. The knowledge estimate pooled 107 studies and 25,492 participants.","context":"Mean difference 11.90/100 (95% CI 10.60–13.19), high-certainty evidence for this outcome. Evidence searched to March 2022; aids supplement clinical consultation."},"teachback":{"title":"Teach-back across different settings","numbers":[["20","studies included"],["19","reported benefit on at least one outcome"]],"kind":"none","source":"Talevski et al. · PLOS ONE · 2020","url":"https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0231350","type":"Systematic review","finding":"Teach-back showed promising results, with varied methods and outcomes.","population":"Studies across different populations and care settings, searched to July 2019.","context":"Counts of studies, not a 95% treatment benefit. Seven studies were rated weak and outcomes were heterogeneous; no single pooled clinical effect is claimed."},"empathy":{"title":"Empathy and positive communication","numbers":[["28","trials in the full review"],["6,017","participants overall"]],"kind":"none","source":"Howick et al. · Journal of the Royal Society of Medicine · 2018","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6047264/","type":"Review of randomised trials","finding":"The empathy subgroup found a small improvement in pooled patient outcomes.","population":"Seven trials tested empathic consultations; other trials tested positive expectations.","context":"Empathy subgroup SMD −0.18 (95% CI −0.32 to −0.03). This standardised effect is not a percentage reduction. Conditions, outcomes and certainty varied."},"interruptions":{"title":"Interruptions during medication work","numbers":[["53.1%","of observed administrations were interrupted"],["4,271","medication administrations observed"]],"kind":"bar","ratio":53.1,"source":"Westbrook et al. · Archives of Internal Medicine · 2010","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20421552/","type":"Direct observational study","finding":"More interruptions were associated with more errors in the observed setting.","population":"98 nurses, six wards, two Sydney teaching hospitals; 2006–2008 observations.","context":"Each interruption was associated with a 12.7% increase in clinical errors in the study model. Observational association, not a universal error probability or a rationale to delay urgent communication."},"digital":{"title":"A citation can look real and still be false","numbers":[["55%","fabricated citations in the GPT-3.5 sample"],["18%","fabricated citations in the GPT-4 sample"]],"kind":"compare","ratio":32.72727272727273,"source":"Walters & Wilder · Scientific Reports · 2023","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-41032-5","type":"Model-output benchmark study","finding":"The sampled outputs contained fabricated and erroneous references.","population":"636 citations generated for the study’s prompts using 2023-era models.","context":"Historical benchmark, not a current error rate for all AI systems or clinical outputs. Different prompts, versions and verification workflows can produce different results."},"simulation":{"title":"Why actively rehearse?","numbers":[["609","studies included"],["35,226","health-professions trainees"]],"kind":"none","source":"Cook et al. · JAMA · 2011","url":"https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jama/article-abstract/1104300","type":"Systematic review and meta-analysis","finding":"Simulation training outperformed no intervention on pooled learning outcomes.","population":"Multiple health professions and simulation formats; 137 randomised studies plus other designs.","context":"Large heterogeneity and predominantly pre/post studies. The comparison was no intervention; it does not establish superiority to every teaching method or validate these particular simulations."},"audit":{"title":"Feedback can support better practice","numbers":[["+6.2","percentage-point mean improvement"],["292","studies in the full review"]],"kind":"none","source":"Ivers et al. · Cochrane · 2025","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40130784/","type":"Systematic review","finding":"Audit and feedback improved desired professional practice on average.","population":"The mean estimate pooled 177 studies comparing audit and feedback with control.","context":"95% CI 4.1–8.2 percentage points; moderate-certainty evidence. Effects varied and interventions often included education or reminders. This is not an effect of informal feedback alone."}};
const CC_TOPIC_EVIDENCE={"communication":"teachback","safety":"medication","empathy":"empathy","sbar":"handoff","infection":"ipc","reasoning":"harm","speakup":"handoff","shared":"engagement","ai":"digital","wellbeing":"wellbeing","equity":"access","improvement":"audit","health-literacy":"teachback","risk-evidence":"engagement","human-factors":"interruptions","med-transitions":"medication","deterioration":"handoff","data-stewardship":"digital","teachback-skill":"teachback","conflict":"empathy","documentation":"handoff","risk-dialogue":"engagement","feedback":"audit","debrief":"simulation","humility":"harm","dignity":"access","just-culture":"harm","accountability":"handoff","compassion-boundaries":"wellbeing","inclusive-leadership":"access"};
const CC_SIMS=[{"id":"sim-handover","title":"The handover control room","image":"handover","lesson":"sbar","evidence":"handoff","theme":"bedside","description":"Explore a 3D room, locate three communication gaps and make the next action explicit.","why":"A handover needs a shared action, named responsibility and a contingency.","when":"Before practising a handover or facilitating a team rehearsal.","how":"Explore all three hotspots, choose a response at each and review your decision trail.","zones":[{"name":"The bedside change","label":"Observe","x":102,"y":135,"copy":"The fictional handover says “stable”, but the new observation in the scenario does not fit that label. No patient measurements or treatment decisions are simulated.","question":"What should the message lead with?","answers":["The observed change and your concern, with urgency communicated through the local route.","The earlier reassuring label, without mentioning the change.","A complete historical narrative before any concern."],"correct":0,"why":["A current change should be explicit. Urgent concerns require the immediate local pathway.","An earlier label does not resolve new information.","Relevant background matters, but must not obscure or delay a significant concern."]},{"name":"The responsibility board","label":"Assign","x":305,"y":78,"copy":"The fictional receiving team has acknowledged the concern. The review task still reads “team aware” and has no named owner.","question":"What remains to be agreed?","answers":["That the message was long enough.","Who will act, the agreed timing and how responsibility is accepted.","That everyone in the room has received a copy."],"correct":1,"why":["Length does not establish ownership.","An explicit owner and timing turn acknowledgment into an actionable plan.","More recipients can still leave responsibility unassigned."]},{"name":"The communication station","label":"Close","x":290,"y":205,"copy":"A named person has accepted the action. The fictional team has not agreed what happens if the review is delayed or the situation changes.","question":"Which closing action is useful?","answers":["Assume the original plan will happen.","Document the concern and end the discussion.","Confirm the contingency and local re-escalation route, alongside the approved record."],"correct":2,"why":["A plan also needs a response to delay or change.","Documentation supports communication but does not establish the contingency.","Closing the loop includes what to do when the plan cannot be carried out."]}]},{"id":"sim-interruptions","title":"The interruption recovery desk","image":"interruptions","lesson":"human-factors","evidence":"interruptions","theme":"desk","description":"Inspect a task desk, distinguish competing demands and recover a verified task state.","why":"An interruption can leave task status, urgency and ownership uncertain.","when":"When reviewing interruption-prone work or planning a safer task handoff.","how":"Inspect the task record, incoming demand and return point. Choose a response at each hotspot.","zones":[{"name":"The task record","label":"Verify","x":170,"y":95,"copy":"A fictional coordination task was interrupted. A checklist is open, but you do not remember which parts were completed. This is a communication exercise, not medication preparation.","question":"What is the useful starting point?","answers":["Resume where you think you were.","Check the approved task record and establish completed and outstanding steps.","Mark the task complete to prevent duplication."],"correct":1,"why":["Memory alone may not reveal the actual task state.","A verified task state is a stronger basis for resuming than an assumption.","Unverified completion can hide outstanding work."]},{"name":"The incoming demand","label":"Prioritise","x":318,"y":170,"copy":"A colleague interrupts with a request. The scenario does not establish whether the request is urgent. “Never interrupt” would be an unsafe blanket rule.","question":"What should you clarify?","answers":["The request’s urgency and appropriate response, using local escalation where indicated.","Whether the colleague can wait in every circumstance.","Whether ignoring requests will make the queue smaller."],"correct":0,"why":["Urgent concerns must not be blocked by a task-protection habit. Clarify the actual need.","Some requests require immediate attention.","Silence does not resolve urgency or responsibility."]},{"name":"The return tray","label":"Resume","x":90,"y":208,"copy":"You have addressed the incoming demand. A second person may have moved the original task forward while you were away.","question":"How do you avoid a silent gap or duplicate work?","answers":["Restart every step regardless of what others did.","Continue from the original memory of your place.","Confirm the current status and owner, then resume from the verified point."],"correct":2,"why":["A full restart may introduce unnecessary duplication; first establish the state.","The task may have changed while you were away.","A status-and-ownership check supports a deliberate return."]}]},{"id":"sim-shared-choice","title":"The shared-choice consultation","image":"shared-decision","lesson":"shared","evidence":"engagement","theme":"conversation","description":"Explore a consultation space, compare fictional numbers and bring the person’s priorities into the plan.","why":"A balanced comparison needs evidence, uncertainty and the person’s practical priorities.","when":"Before rehearsing a shared-decision or risk-explanation conversation.","how":"Explore the person’s priorities, the number board and the follow-up plan. The displayed numbers are synthetic.","zones":[{"name":"The person’s priorities","label":"Ask","x":82,"y":155,"copy":"In a fictional conversation, two possible plans have different visit and preparation burdens. The person has not been asked what would be difficult or important to them.","question":"Which question makes the comparison more useful?","answers":["Which plan does the team prefer?","What matters most to you, and what would make either plan difficult?","Which option is most popular?"],"correct":1,"why":["Team preference alone does not establish the person’s priorities.","A preference-and-barrier question helps connect options to the individual.","A popular option may not fit this person."]},{"name":"The fictional number board","label":"Compare","x":200,"y":72,"copy":"Synthetic example: the same outcome occurs in 8 of 100 people with one option and 5 of 100 with another, over the same period. No treatment, real-world rate or causal claim is implied.","question":"Which explanation keeps the absolute difference visible?","answers":["That is 3 fewer in 100, or 3 percentage points, alongside burdens and uncertainty.","That is 37.5 fewer people in every 100.","The second option guarantees a better outcome for this person."],"correct":0,"why":["8/100 minus 5/100 is 3/100. The baseline, time period and trade-offs remain important.","37.5% is the relative reduction in this synthetic example, not the absolute number in 100.","A group-level probability does not guarantee an individual result."]},{"name":"The shared next step","label":"Agree","x":310,"y":180,"copy":"The person wants time to consider the options and discuss a practical barrier. The plan is not yet agreed.","question":"What supports an informed next step?","answers":["Record agreement because the information has been provided.","Choose the option with the larger-looking percentage.","Clarify the support needed and agree appropriate follow-up with the responsible clinician."],"correct":2,"why":["Receiving information is not the same as reaching a shared agreement.","A percentage alone cannot resolve priorities and uncertainty.","Support, ownership and follow-up help the decision remain informed and revisable."]}]}];
const CC_USE={"communication":["Anyone explaining care or a next step.","Before an explanation, discharge conversation or follow-up discussion."],"safety":["Care practitioners and teams managing transitions.","When a record, instruction or intended action is unclear."],"empathy":["Anyone speaking with a person about their care.","When a concern, emotion or practical barrier needs to be understood."],"sbar":["Colleagues transferring information and responsibility.","At a handover or when raising a change or unresolved concern."],"infection":["People involved in care and shared equipment.","When planning precautions or moving between care tasks."],"reasoning":["Practitioners and learners reviewing an explanation.","When new information does not fit the first interpretation."],"speakup":["Every team member, including students and supervisors.","When raising a concern or receiving a colleague’s question."],"shared":["Care teams and people considering genuine options.","When options involve different benefits, burdens or priorities."],"ai":["Staff and learners using approved digital workflows.","Before relying on a generated claim, summary or citation."],"wellbeing":["Healthcare workers, peers and supervisors.","When strain, recovery or working conditions need attention."],"equity":["Teams planning an accessible conversation or service.","When the usual format may not meet a person’s needs."],"improvement":["Teams and facilitators planning an approved test.","When a recurring process gap needs a measurable change."],"health-literacy":["Anyone explaining information or service instructions.","When jargon, format or navigation makes a next step hard to use."],"risk-evidence":["People interpreting studies or explaining probabilities.","When a percentage is presented without its baseline or time period."],"human-factors":["People reviewing how work actually happens.","When interruptions, memory load or unclear cues make a task fragile."],"med-transitions":["Teams involved in medication reconciliation.","When medication accounts differ at a transfer or transition."],"deterioration":["Care teams practising recognition and escalation.","When a current change conflicts with an earlier reassuring label."],"data-stewardship":["Anyone handling confidential or generated information.","Before sharing material or choosing a digital destination."],"teachback-skill":["Practitioners and learners rehearsing an explanation.","When checking and repairing a gap in shared understanding."],"conflict":["Colleagues seeking a useful response to disagreement.","When personal friction is obscuring an observable concern."],"documentation":["People writing an actionable care or learning record.","When the next colleague needs clear facts, ownership and timing."],"risk-dialogue":["People rehearsing a balanced options conversation.","When numbers need to be connected to the person’s priorities."],"feedback":["Peers, supervisors, educators and learners.","When a specific observation can inform a manageable next step."],"debrief":["Teams and facilitators reflecting on a fictional event.","After a rehearsal, event or task with useful learning to discuss."],"humility":["People reviewing a working explanation or assumption.","When another perspective or new information could change the view."],"dignity":["Anyone participating in a care conversation.","When permission, privacy or the person’s voice needs attention."],"just-culture":["Teams reviewing a near miss or process failure.","When facts, context and fair accountability need to be considered together."],"accountability":["Anyone accepting, handing over or following up a task.","When a commitment needs an owner, timing or a contingency."],"compassion-boundaries":["Colleagues and practitioners offering practical support.","When a supportive response also needs an honest boundary."],"inclusive-leadership":["Team members, facilitators and people leading a discussion.","When a decision is missing a role, perspective or accessible route for input."]};
const CC_ART_ICONS={"communication":"<path d=\"M4 4h16v12H9l-5 4zM8 8h8M8 12h5\"/>","safety":"<path d=\"m12 2 8 3v6c0 6-8 11-8 11S4 17 4 11V5zM8 12l3 3 5-6\"/>","empathy":"<path d=\"M12 20 4 12C-1 7 6 1 12 7c6-6 13 0 8 5zM7 11h3l2-3 2 6 2-3h2\"/>","sbar":"<rect x=\"3\" y=\"3\" width=\"18\" height=\"18\" rx=\"3\"/><path d=\"M7 7h3M14 7h3M7 12h10M7 17h6\"/>","infection":"<path d=\"M12 2v4M12 18v4M2 12h4M18 12h4M5 5l3 3M16 16l3 3M5 19l3-3M16 8l3-3\"/><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"6\"/><path d=\"m9 12 2 2 4-4\"/>","reasoning":"<circle cx=\"10\" cy=\"10\" r=\"7\"/><path d=\"m15 15 6 6M7 10h6M10 7v6\"/>","speakup":"<path d=\"m3 10 13-5v14L3 14zM16 9l5-2v10l-5-2M5 15l2 6h4l-2-5M20 2l-2 2M22 20l-3-2\"/>","shared":"<circle cx=\"6\" cy=\"5\" r=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"18\" cy=\"5\" r=\"2\"/><path d=\"M2 15v-3a4 4 0 0 1 8 0v3M14 15v-3a4 4 0 0 1 8 0v3M7 19h10m-3-3 3 3-3 3\"/>","ai":"<rect x=\"4\" y=\"4\" width=\"16\" height=\"16\" rx=\"4\"/><path d=\"M8 8h8v8H8zM8 1v3M16 1v3M8 20v3M16 20v3M1 8h3M1 16h3M20 8h3M20 16h3\"/>","wellbeing":"<path d=\"M3 20c1-10 6-15 18-16 0 12-5 17-15 16M3 22 16 9M9 16v-5M13 12h5\"/>","equity":"<path d=\"M3 20h18M5 20V8h5v12M14 20V4h5v16M5 4h5M14 1h5M3 12h9\"/>","improvement":"<path d=\"M19 6a9 9 0 1 0 2 10M19 2v6h-6M7 15l4-4 3 2 5-5\"/>","health-literacy":"<path d=\"M12 6v15M3 4h5l4 2 4-2h5v15h-5l-4 2-4-2H3zM6 8h2M6 12h2M16 8h2M16 12h2\"/>","risk-evidence":"<path d=\"M3 3v18h18M7 17v-5M12 17V7M17 17v-8M7 8l5-5 5 3\"/>","human-factors":"<circle cx=\"9\" cy=\"5\" r=\"3\"/><path d=\"M3 20v-4a6 6 0 0 1 10-4M16 11v3m0 6v3M10 17h3m6 0h3\"/><circle cx=\"16\" cy=\"17\" r=\"3\"/>","med-transitions":"<path d=\"m6 4 14 14M4 6a5 5 0 0 1 7-2l9 9a5 5 0 0 1-7 7l-9-9a5 5 0 0 1 0-7M2 20h7m-3-3 3 3-3 3\"/>","deterioration":"<path d=\"M2 12h5l3-8 4 16 3-8h5M4 3h3M17 21h3\"/>","data-stewardship":"<rect x=\"4\" y=\"10\" width=\"16\" height=\"12\" rx=\"2\"/><path d=\"M7 10V7a5 5 0 0 1 10 0v3M12 15v3\"/>","teachback-skill":"<path d=\"M4 7h14l-4-4M18 17H4l4 4M20 7v5M2 17v-5\"/><circle cx=\"11\" cy=\"12\" r=\"2\"/>","conflict":"<path d=\"M2 3h13v9H7l-5 4zM18 8h4v12l-5-4h-6v-2M6 7h5M17 12h2\"/>","documentation":"<path d=\"M5 2h10l4 4v16H5zM15 2v5h4M8 11h8M8 15h8M8 19h5\"/>","risk-dialogue":"<path d=\"M3 3h18v13H9l-6 5zM7 12V8M12 12V6M17 12V9\"/>","feedback":"<path d=\"M3 3h18v14H9l-6 5zM7 10l3 3 7-7\"/>","debrief":"<circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"4\"/><circle cx=\"5\" cy=\"4\" r=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"19\" cy=\"4\" r=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"21\" r=\"2\"/><path d=\"m7 6 2 3M17 6l-2 3M12 16v3\"/>","humility":"<path d=\"M7 5a8 8 0 1 1-2 11M7 1v6H1M10 10a2 2 0 1 1 3 2c-1 .5-1 1-1 2M12 17h.01\"/>","dignity":"<circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"6\" r=\"4\"/><path d=\"M4 22v-4a8 8 0 0 1 16 0v4M8 16l4 3 4-3\"/>","just-culture":"<path d=\"M12 2v20M5 22h14M3 6h18M5 6l-3 8h6zM19 6l-3 8h6z\"/>","accountability":"<rect x=\"4\" y=\"4\" width=\"16\" height=\"18\" rx=\"2\"/><path d=\"M9 2h6v4H9zM8 11l2 2 5-5M8 17h8\"/>","compassion-boundaries":"<path d=\"M3 3v18M21 3v18M12 18l-6-6c-4-4 2-8 6-3 4-5 10-1 6 3z\"/>","inclusive-leadership":"<circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"4\" r=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"4\" cy=\"12\" r=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"20\" cy=\"12\" r=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"20\" r=\"2\"/><path d=\"m6 6 3 3M15 9l3-3M6 18l3-3M15 15l3 3\"/>","room":"<path d=\"m3 6 9-4 9 4v12l-9 4-9-4zM3 6l9 5 9-5M12 11v11M6 15l3 1\"/>","movement":"<circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"4\" r=\"2\"/><path d=\"m5 10 7-3 7 3M12 7v7m0 0-5 7m5-7 5 7\"/>","flow":"<rect x=\"2\" y=\"3\" width=\"6\" height=\"6\" rx=\"1\"/><rect x=\"16\" y=\"15\" width=\"6\" height=\"6\" rx=\"1\"/><path d=\"M5 9v9h11M13 15l3 3-3 3M11 6h8v6M16 9l3 3 3-3\"/>","interruptions":"<path d=\"M4 3h11v18H4zM7 7h5M7 11h5M7 15h3M18 3v8M18 15h.01M16 21h6\"/>"};
const HCA_ROOM_ZONES=[["Patient zone","Patient contact and the immediate surroundings are distinct contexts. Use the vignette to identify what happened."],["Clean-task tray","A clean or aseptic task has its own hand-hygiene indication. The illustration does not demonstrate a procedure."],["Exposure-risk task","Assess the exposure described in the vignette. The markers do not detect or represent contamination."],["Bedside surroundings","Contact with patient surroundings can matter even when there has been no direct patient contact."],["Hand-hygiene station","Use the appropriate method under current local policy. Gloves do not replace indicated hand hygiene."]];
const CC_STATIC_CONTENT={"about":"<p>Carecraft connects 30 lessons, 90 practice prompts, four branching cases, expanded Interactive Studios and practical workspaces. Learn a principle, rehearse it and return for review.</p><div class=\"hca-how-grid\"><section><h3>Learn.</h3><p>Understand a principle through a clear explanation and a worked example.</p></section><section><h3>Practise.</h3><p>Explore a fictional decision, see its consequences and understand the alternatives.</p></section><section><h3>Return.</h3><p>Revisit a prompt, reflect on an action and keep a record of your learning.</p></section></div><h3>What your records mean</h3><p>Activity completion and self-reported reflections are not clinical competence, certification or accredited CPD/CME. Content has not undergone independent clinical peer review.</p><p>Created by Hossam AlDhaher, LSSMBB.</p><h3>One academy. A shared identity.</h3><p>The original academy crest family, navy, teal, copper and ivory palette, Playfair Display and Inter typography are used with the supplied Healthcare Crown Academy brand kit.</p><div class=\"hca-trust\"><a href=\"https://ico.org.uk/ESDWebPages/Entry/ZC273259\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><span class=\"hca-icon\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\"><path d=\"m12 3 8 3v6c0 5-8 10-8 10S4 17 4 12V6zM8 12l3 3 5-6\"></path></svg></span><span>ICO registration <b>ZC273259</b> ↗</span></a><a href=\"https://healthcarecrownacademy.co.uk\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Healthcare Crown Academy ↗</a></div><div class=\"modal-actions\"><button class=\"btn secondary\" data-action=\"engine-92\" data-engine-92=\"[]\">Download this app</button><button class=\"btn\" data-action=\"engine-93\" data-engine-93=\"[]\">Explore learning →</button></div>","privacy":"<p>This app saves your optional first name, role, learning goal, completed lessons, scenario activity, bookmarks, self-check, studio activities and reflections, and appearance preference in this browser’s local storage. It has no server-side account, cloud sync, or analytics.</p><h3>Use a trusted device</h3><p>Other people using the same browser profile may see your learning notes. Do not record patient, colleague, or confidential information. Clearing browser data removes your saved progress. Exported files stay on the device where you download them.</p><h3>External sources</h3><p>Source links open external websites under their own privacy policies. Images and fonts in the downloadable app are embedded, so the app does not need third-party asset requests.</p><div class=\"modal-actions\"><button class=\"btn secondary\" data-action=\"engine-94\" data-engine-94=\"[]\">Clear my saved data</button><button class=\"btn\" data-action=\"engine-28\" data-engine-28=\"[]\">Understood</button></div><h3>Your Phase 2 learning records</h3><p>Action plans, review dates and notes, lesson-check choices, fictional scenario trails, and tool drafts are also saved in this browser. Review prompts appear only inside the app when you return; no reminders or notifications are sent. The app does not validate clinical competence or verify your real-world actions.</p><h3>Carecraft version 2</h3><p>Your learning focus, setting, motion preference, latest session, recall review dates and individual Studio attempts are also stored in this browser. The review queue is shown only when you open the app; it sends no notifications. The current app has no analytics, accounts or cloud sync.</p><p>Backups contain your learning data and notes. Store them privately. A backup can restore your workspace; restoring replaces this browser’s existing Carecraft data.</p><div class=\"hca-backup-row\"><button class=\"btn secondary\" data-action=\"engine-95\" data-engine-95=\"[]\">Download backup</button><button class=\"btn secondary\" data-action=\"engine-96\" data-engine-96=\"[]\">Restore backup</button></div><p>Company: HEALTHCARE CROWN ACADEMY LTD, 71–75 Shelton Street, Covent Garden, London WC2H 9JQ. Company no. 17501904. ICO registration ZC273259, supplied in the company brand kit.</p>","sources":"<p>Lessons summarize established cross-disciplinary principles rather than specialty treatment protocols. Supporting links appear in each lesson. Source publication dates vary; this is not a claim that every source is newly published or that this app is continuously updated.</p><div class=\"learning-step\"><div><strong>AHRQ · TeamSTEPPS 3.0 tools and demonstrations</strong><p style=\"overflow-wrap:anywhere\"><a href=\"https://www.ahrq.gov/teamstepps-program/resources/additional/index.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open source ↗</a></p></div></div><div class=\"learning-step\"><div><strong>AHRQ · SHARE: essential steps of shared decision-making</strong><p style=\"overflow-wrap:anywhere\"><a href=\"https://www.ahrq.gov/health-literacy/professional-training/shared-decision/tool/resource-1.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open source ↗</a></p></div></div><div class=\"learning-step\"><div><strong>WHO · Medication Without Harm</strong><p style=\"overflow-wrap:anywhere\"><a href=\"https://www.who.int/initiatives/medication-without-harm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open source ↗</a></p></div></div><div class=\"learning-step\"><div><strong>CDC · Standard and transmission-based precautions</strong><p style=\"overflow-wrap:anywhere\"><a href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/infection-control/hcp/isolation-precautions/precautions.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open source ↗</a></p></div></div><div class=\"learning-step\"><div><strong>WHO · AI ethics and governance guidance (2024)</strong><p style=\"overflow-wrap:anywhere\"><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news/item/18-01-2024-who-releases-ai-ethics-and-governance-guidance-for-large-multi-modal-models\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open source ↗</a></p></div></div><div class=\"learning-step\"><div><strong>WHO · Patient safety (2023)</strong><p style=\"overflow-wrap:anywhere\"><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/patient-safety\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open source ↗</a></p></div></div><div class=\"learning-step\"><div><strong>AHRQ · Health Literacy Universal Precautions Toolkit, 3rd edition (2024)</strong><p style=\"overflow-wrap:anywhere\"><a href=\"https://www.ahrq.gov/health-literacy/improve/precautions/toolkit.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open source ↗</a></p></div></div><div class=\"learning-step\"><div><strong>WHO · Disability (2023)</strong><p style=\"overflow-wrap:anywhere\"><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open source ↗</a></p></div></div><div class=\"learning-step\"><div><strong>WHO · Mental health at work (2026)</strong><p style=\"overflow-wrap:anywhere\"><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-at-work\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open source ↗</a></p></div></div><div class=\"learning-step\"><div><strong>Talevski et al. · PLOS ONE · 2020</strong><p style=\"overflow-wrap:anywhere\"><a href=\"https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0231350\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open source ↗</a></p></div></div><div class=\"learning-step\"><div><strong>Schnipper et al. · BMJ Quality &amp; Safety · 2022</strong><p style=\"overflow-wrap:anywhere\"><a href=\"https://qualitysafety.bmj.com/content/31/4/278\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open source ↗</a></p></div></div><div class=\"learning-step\"><div><strong>Howick et al. · Journal of the Royal Society of Medicine · 2018</strong><p style=\"overflow-wrap:anywhere\"><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6047264/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open source ↗</a></p></div></div><div class=\"learning-step\"><div><strong>Starmer et al. · NEJM · 2014</strong><p style=\"overflow-wrap:anywhere\"><a href=\"https://www.nejm.org/doi/abs/10.1056/NEJMsa1405556\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open source ↗</a></p></div></div><div class=\"learning-step\"><div><strong>Pronovost et al. · NEJM · 2006</strong><p style=\"overflow-wrap:anywhere\"><a href=\"https://www.nejm.org/doi/abs/10.1056/NEJMoa061115\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open source ↗</a></p></div></div><div class=\"learning-step\"><div><strong>Panagioti et al. · BMJ · 2019</strong><p style=\"overflow-wrap:anywhere\"><a href=\"https://www.bmj.com/content/366/bmj.l4185\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open source ↗</a></p></div></div><div class=\"learning-step\"><div><strong>Stacey et al. · Cochrane · 2024</strong><p style=\"overflow-wrap:anywhere\"><a href=\"https://www.cochrane.org/evidence/CD001431_patient-decision-aids-help-people-who-are-facing-decisions-about-health-treatment-or-screening\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open source ↗</a></p></div></div><div class=\"learning-step\"><div><strong>Walters &amp; Wilder · Scientific Reports · 2023</strong><p style=\"overflow-wrap:anywhere\"><a href=\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-41032-5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open source ↗</a></p></div></div><div class=\"learning-step\"><div><strong>Hodkinson et al. · BMJ · 2022</strong><p style=\"overflow-wrap:anywhere\"><a href=\"https://www.bmj.com/content/378/bmj-2022-070442\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open source ↗</a></p></div></div><div class=\"learning-step\"><div><strong>CDC · Disability and Health Data Now · 2025</strong><p style=\"overflow-wrap:anywhere\"><a href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/disability-and-health/articles-documents/disability-and-health-data-now.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open source ↗</a></p></div></div><div class=\"learning-step\"><div><strong>Ivers et al. · Cochrane · 2025</strong><p style=\"overflow-wrap:anywhere\"><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40130784/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open source ↗</a></p></div></div><div class=\"learning-step\"><div><strong>Westbrook et al. · Archives of Internal Medicine · 2010</strong><p style=\"overflow-wrap:anywhere\"><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20421552/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open source ↗</a></p></div></div><div class=\"learning-step\"><div><strong>Cook et al. · JAMA · 2011</strong><p style=\"overflow-wrap:anywhere\"><a href=\"https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jama/article-abstract/1104300\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open source ↗</a></p></div></div><h3>Editorial transparency</h3><p>Clinical reasoning, quality-improvement, appraisal, and wellbeing sections include general educational synthesis. Scenario wording and quotes are original. No professional accreditation, independent clinical review, affiliation, or endorsement is implied.</p><h3>Image &amp; type credits</h3><p>The collaborative clinical-team image is AI-generated. Supporting photographic images are from Unsplash (image identifiers: 1576091160399-112ba8d25d1d, 1584982751601-97dcc096659c, 1472396961693-142e6e269027). DM Sans and Manrope are open-source typefaces supplied through Google Fonts. Images are illustrative, not evidence or depictions of actual participants.</p><div class=\"notice\" style=\"margin-top:18px\">Before using this app in formal staff education, obtain local clinical review, check applicable policies and source updates, and review accessibility, licensing, and governance requirements.</div><h3>Phase 2 editorial additions</h3><p>The worked examples, answer explanations, branching scenarios, and planning aids are original fictional educational material. They have not undergone independent clinical peer review. Supporting principles include AHRQ SBAR, CDC standard precautions, and WHO AI governance guidance. The handover photograph was AI-generated for this app and depicts fictional people.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/infection-control/hcp/basics/standard-precautions.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CDC: Standard Precautions for All Patient Care ↗</a></p><h3>Interactive expansion references</h3><p>Public image and web searches informed the choice of familiar learning patterns: scenario decisions, message confirmation, structured team boards, and improvement templates. Carecraft’s layout, examples, animation, and code are original. No third-party screenshots, illustrations, videos, logos, or proprietary game assets are embedded.</p><div class=\"adv-source\">Original Carecraft educational activity. Inspired by the structure of <a href=\"https://www.ahrq.gov/teamstepps-program/curriculum/mutual/tools/cus.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">AHRQ CUS ↗</a>. No affiliation, endorsement, or independently validated score is implied.</div><div class=\"adv-source\">Original Carecraft educational activity. Inspired by the structure of <a href=\"https://www.ahrq.gov/teamstepps-program/curriculum/mutual/tools/desc.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">AHRQ DESC ↗</a>. No affiliation, endorsement, or independently validated score is implied.</div><div class=\"adv-source\">Original Carecraft educational activity. Inspired by the structure of <a href=\"https://www.ahrq.gov/teamstepps-program/curriculum/team/tools/index.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">AHRQ team leadership tools ↗</a>. No affiliation, endorsement, or independently validated score is implied.</div><div class=\"adv-source\">Original Carecraft educational activity. Inspired by the structure of <a href=\"https://www.ihi.org/library/tools/quality-improvement-essentials-toolkit\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">IHI quality-improvement tools ↗</a>. No affiliation, endorsement, or independently validated score is implied.</div><p>Additional format inspiration: <a href=\"https://5mgame.lxp.academy.who.int/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">WHO My 5 Moments game</a> and <a href=\"https://www.resus.org.uk/public-resource/how-we-save-lives/lifesaver-learning\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">RCUK Lifesaver</a>. These references are examples, not a popularity ranking.</p><h3>Carecraft / Healthcare Crown Academy edition</h3><p>The HCA visual identity uses the supplied crest family and self-hosted Playfair Display and Inter. Photographs retain the original app’s image credits. The care-room explorer is original CSS 3D geometry, not a clinical procedure animation. New question variants and scenario finales are original educational practice and have not undergone independent clinical peer review.</p><p>No certificate, registration security number, payment details or private brand-kit document is embedded in this app.</p>"};
/* Topic-specific teaching notes extend the canonical three-step reading. */
const LESSON_NOTES={
 communication:['Notice whether the person has understood the purpose of the conversation, not just heard the words. A calm opening creates room for their concern before you explain yours.','When a response sounds reassuring but vague, ask what the person expects to happen next. Summarise the agreed action in ordinary language and invite a correction.'],
 safety:['A reliable check links the right person, current record and intended action. Treat an unexplained mismatch as information to resolve before moving on.','At a transition, name who will clarify a discrepancy and where the corrected plan will be recorded. A near miss is useful learning only when the system can see it.'],
 empathy:['Empathy begins with a specific observation or question, then leaves space for the answer. It does not require guessing how someone feels.','Acknowledge the impact you hear, explain what you can do within your role, and make the next contact or action clear. Notice when an assumption has replaced curiosity.'],
 sbar:['Prepare the one decision or response you need before choosing a format. Situation and background should orient the listener; assessment and recommendation make the next step actionable.','Close the loop by confirming who will act, what they understood and when you will reconnect. If the concern remains unresolved, use the local escalation route.'],
 infection:['Think of prevention as a sequence around a task rather than a single ritual. The right action depends on the point of care, exposure risk and local guidance.','Watch for the moment an apparently clean workflow changes: touching a shared surface, moving between tasks or removing protection. Pause and reset the sequence before continuing.'],
 reasoning:['Separate an observation from your interpretation of it. Write down what is known, what is uncertain and what finding would change your working view.','A second plausible explanation is a prompt to seek information, not a reason to delay needed escalation. Revisit the working view when new evidence arrives.'],
 speakup:['A concern is easier to act on when it names an observation, its possible consequence and a request. Use plain language without implying certainty you do not have.','If the first attempt is not heard, repeat the concern more directly and follow the escalation pathway. Afterwards, reflect on whether the team made speaking up easier or harder.'],
 shared:['Begin with what matters to the person and which options are actually available. Explain the tradeoffs in a way they can use, including uncertainty.','A preference expressed after an explanation may differ from the first answer. Check understanding and document the decision and any follow-up question.'],
 ai:['Treat generated output as an unverified draft. Ask what data it used, what might be missing and whether the proposed action is supported by an accountable source.','Protect confidential information under local rules and verify important claims independently. Responsibility for a clinical decision remains with the authorised human team.'],
 wellbeing:['A brief pause can make a demand easier to notice, but it does not fix an unsafe workload. Distinguish what you can change now from what needs team or organisational action.','Name a practical boundary, request help early and use available support. Reflect without turning normal strain into a personal failure.'],
 equity:['Ask what barriers are present in this encounter rather than assuming they follow from identity. Access, language, time and trust can all alter what a reasonable plan looks like.','Offer a way to correct your understanding and check whether the proposed next step is feasible. Escalate structural barriers through the appropriate service route.'],
 improvement:['Define a problem as an observable process before choosing a solution. A small test should specify what changed, where, and how you will know whether it helped.','Look for unintended effects as well as the desired outcome. Share what failed, adjust the idea and measure again before declaring a new standard.'],
 'health-literacy':['The burden of making an explanation understandable belongs with the service, not with the learner or patient. Replace jargon and divide a plan into manageable actions.','Ask the person to describe the plan in their own words and repair any gap without blame. An interpreter or accessible format may be needed for a fair conversation.'],
 'risk-evidence':['A percentage has no meaning without its denominator, time period and population. Ask whether the comparison is absolute or relative and whether the groups resemble the person in front of you.','Describe benefits and harms on the same scale when possible. If the evidence does not apply cleanly, state that uncertainty rather than presenting a false precision.'],
 'human-factors':['Design a workflow for real interruptions, limited attention and handoffs. A reminder helps only if the right person sees it at the right decision point.','When an error occurs, examine conditions and signals before attributing it to carelessness. Test a system change with the people who do the work.'],
 'med-transitions':['A list copied forward can appear complete while missing a recent change. Compare the best available history with the intended plan and identify who can confirm a discrepancy.','Make additions, changes and stops visible to the next team and to the person taking the medicines. Follow local reconciliation and prescribing responsibilities.'],
 deterioration:['Notice a meaningful change from the person’s baseline as well as a single measurement. Combine the observation, trend and concern in an escalation that requests a response.','Reassess while help is being arranged according to local protocol. Confirm who has accepted responsibility and document the handover and response.'],
 'data-stewardship':['Before collecting or sharing information, ask whether it is needed for the task and whether the recipient is authorised. Convenience is not a reason to widen access.','Use approved systems, check the recipient and report a suspected breach promptly through local channels. Explain data use honestly when the person asks.'],
 'teachback-skill':['Teach-back checks the clarity of your explanation. Frame it as your responsibility: ask the person to show how they will use the plan, rather than testing them.','If their account differs, change your wording or medium and try again. Confirm the action and where they can seek help if circumstances change.'],
 conflict:['Identify the shared purpose before debating a proposed action. Describe the disputed observation and its impact without assigning motives.','Invite the other perspective, then agree on a safe next step and ownership. If the disagreement creates an immediate risk, use the appropriate escalation route.'],
 documentation:['Write for the next person who must understand what happened and what to do. Distinguish observed facts, decisions, outstanding questions and named follow-up.','Check that timing, authorship and amendments are clear under local policy. Avoid vague reassurance when a concern still needs review.'],
 'risk-dialogue':['Start with the decision the person faces and the outcomes they care about. Present comparable options and make uncertainty explicit.','Check whether the numbers are understood and whether the choice is workable in their context. Record the agreed plan and a route to revisit it.'],
 feedback:['Useful feedback describes an observable action and its effect, then invites the recipient’s view. Keep the focus on a change that can be practised.','Agree on the next attempt and when to review it. A broad label about someone’s character is much harder to act on than a specific example.'],
 debrief:['Create a short, respectful account of what happened before deciding what should change. Let people describe the conditions they saw, including missing information.','Separate immediate support from later system learning. Record an owner and a review date for any proposed improvement so the discussion leads somewhere.'],
 humility:['Confidence can help action, but certainty should remain open to new facts. State what would change your mind and invite a view that differs from yours.','When corrected, update the plan visibly and credit the contribution. This makes it safer for others to question a decision next time.'],
 dignity:['Privacy, language and choice shape the experience of a routine task. Ask permission, explain what will happen and notice who else can hear or see.','If urgency limits choice, explain the reason and restore as much control as possible. Small acts of respect are part of the workflow, not a separate extra.'],
 'just-culture':['Describe what the system made easy or difficult before judging an individual action. Distinguish an honest mistake from a deliberate choice using a fair local process.','Support those affected and create a route for learning that does not hide the event. A proposed fix should address the conditions that contributed to it.'],
 accountability:['Owning an action means naming its scope, the person responsible and the time for review. Make dependencies visible when the next step belongs to another role.','If a commitment cannot be met, communicate early and agree on a revised plan. Follow-through is observable; a promise alone is not.'],
 'compassion-boundaries':['Compassionate care includes a clear, sustainable role. Acknowledge a need, say what you can offer and arrange an appropriate handoff when more support is required.','Notice when repeated extra work hides a service gap. Ask the team to address that gap rather than treating unlimited availability as the standard.'],
 'inclusive-leadership':['Whose perspective is absent from this decision? Invite specific observations from quieter or less senior colleagues before announcing a preferred answer.','Respond constructively when a concern changes the plan. Make ownership and feedback visible so contributors know their input mattered.']
};

